{
  "query": "How should SQL code be aligned to improve readability (the “river” technique)?",
  "answer": "SQL code should be aligned to create a \"river\" effect where keywords are right-aligned and values are left-aligned. This alignment helps readers easily scan over the code by separating keywords from implementation details. Spaces should be used before and after equals (`=`), after commas (`,`), and surrounding apostrophes (`'`) appropriately, such as within parentheses or when not followed by a comma or semicolon. The \"river\" technique involves keeping all keywords aligned to the right-hand side of this river while leaving values and other implementation details left-aligned. This creates a uniform gap down the middle of the query, making it easier to quickly scan over the query definition.",
  "sources": [
    {
